----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART 3 -- the SAME estimator run on SPARC: is the disagreement a property of the DiskMass galaxies?
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  SPARC through the IDENTICAL estimator (exponential disc + exponential gas at 2 R_d, kernel inverted at the measured V(2.2 R_d)): N = 119, median Upsilon_[3.6] = 0.579, spread 0.284 dex
  the same SPARC galaxies with the EXACT mass decomposition instead of the exponential approximation: 0.608 -- so the exponential-disc-plus-gas approximation the DiskMass numbers rely on carries a bias of only -0.022 dex, and is not what separates the two surveys
  like for like, over the band bracket: DiskMass galaxies require Upsilon_[3.6] = 0.448 - 0.582; SPARC galaxies require 0.579.  The two disjoint samples land on the same value.
  [PASS] 120b THE DISAGREEMENT DOES NOT LOCALISE TO THE DISKMASS GALAXIES, which is the item's question answered in the negative and is a stronger statement than a per-galaxy overlap could have made.  Run the identical estimator on SPARC -- disjoint sample, different instrument, different selection, inclinations 30-90 instead of 19-45 -- and it requires the same mass-to-light ratio the DiskMass galaxies do, inside the band-conversion bracket.  So there is nothing special about the galaxies DMS VII chose; the gap to 0.31 is a flat zero-point offset between two ways of weighing a disc, and it would be the same gap on any sample   (DiskMass sample 0.582 in K = 0.448-0.582 at 3.6 um; SPARC 0.579 at 3.6 um, i.e. -0.002 to +0.112 dex apart across the bracket, against the 0.273 dex gap to DiskMass's own value)
  the estimator's own bias, measured on the 106 SPARC galaxies where BOTH estimators run: the single radius at 2.2 R_d gives 0.551, the full-curve fit of item 119 gives 0.539 on the SAME galaxies -- a per-galaxy median bias of -0.005 +- 0.016 dex, i.e. none
  A TRAP AVOIDED, WORTH RECORDING.  Comparing the two estimators' medians over the galaxies each one HAPPENS to accept -- 119 for the single-radius version, 120 for the full-curve one -- gives +0.038 dex, which looks like an estimator bias and is not: it is a difference in which galaxies each estimator can handle.  The per-galaxy comparison on the shared set is the one that means anything.
  [PASS] 120c MY OWN SUSPICION, TESTED AND WITHDRAWN.  This check was written expecting to find that item 17's single-radius estimator reads high against a full-curve fit and that its recorded tension with DiskMass was therefore inflated.  Run properly -- the two estimators on the SAME galaxies rather than on the galaxies each happens to accept -- there is no bias at all: they agree per galaxy to five thousandths of a dex.  Item 17's estimator is vindicated, and so is the like-for-like comparison of check 120b that rests on it.  The lesson is the trap: the same two estimators compared as medians over their own samples differ by 0.04 dex, which would have been reported as an estimator systematic and would have been a sample-selection effect   (on 106 shared SPARC galaxies: 2.2 R_d estimator 0.551 vs full-curve 0.539, per-galaxy median bias -0.005 +- 0.016 dex (-0.3 sigma from zero).  The median-over-different-samples version gives +0.038 dex, 2 times the per-galaxy error, and is spurious.  Item 17's own rotation-curve-form systematic is +-0.096 in Upsilon and remains its dominant one)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART 4 -- localising within the DiskMass sample: which galaxies, and against what
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHAT IS AN IDENTITY HERE, AND WHY IT NEARLY CAUGHT ME.  The required Upsilon is Upsilon = g_bar/A_star with
  g_bar = kernel^-1(g_obs), g_obs built from the deprojected rotation speed V, and A_star built from L_K and h_R.
  So log Upsilon is an ALGEBRAIC function of (V, L_K, h_R) plus nothing.  Correlating the gap with g_obs, g_bar,
  y = g_bar/a_0 or V recovers that identity, not physics: measured here as a demonstration, r(gap, log y) = +0.774 and r(gap, log V) = +0.706.
  The FIRST version of this section reported the two DMS XI asymmetry measures as a 3-4 sigma localisation of the
  gap in rotation-curve QUALITY.  That was wrong and the control below is what caught it: both asymmetry measures
  are strong proxies for the rotation speed itself (r(A_RC, log V) = -0.609, r(A_phi, log V) = -0.679 -- slow discs are
  more asymmetric, which is well known), so their correlation with the gap is the V-identity in disguise.  Both raw
  and partial-at-fixed-V correlations are therefore printed, and only the partial ones are read.

                    variable    N    raw r   sigma  r | log V   sigma   what it would mean
                     log L_K   22   -0.050    -0.2     -0.891    -8.6   ALGEBRA: Upsilon ~ 1/L at fixed V
               log h_R (kpc)   22   -0.147    -0.7     -0.440    -2.1   ALGEBRA: A_star depends on h_R
     mu_0 (R-band, mag/as^2)   22   -0.011    -0.0      0.289     1.3   a surface-density effect?
                  B-K colour   20    0.281     1.2     -0.220    -0.9   a stellar-population effect?
           inclination (deg)   22   -0.361    -1.7     -0.203    -0.9   a deprojection effect?
     inclination error (deg)   22   -0.183    -0.8     -0.030    -0.1   an inclination-quality effect?
              A_phi (DMS XI)   22   -0.564    -3.1     -0.163    -0.7   a kinematic-asymmetry effect?
               A_RC (DMS XI)   22   -0.670    -4.0     -0.428    -2.1   a rotation-curve-quality effect?
  [PASS] 120d THE GAP HAS NO INTERNAL STRUCTURE THAT IS NOT ALGEBRA.  Two of the six candidate variables correlate with it raw at 3-4 sigma -- the survey's own kinematic asymmetry measures -- and both evaporate once the rotation speed is held fixed, because slower discs are more asymmetric and the rotation speed is algebraically inside the gap.  With that partialled out, not one of the six reaches 3 sigma: not surface brightness, not colour, not inclination, not inclination error, not either asymmetry measure.  A flat offset is what an M/L zero-point disagreement looks like, and a flat offset is what is there.  (The two rows marked ALGEBRA are printed as the numerical proof that partialling on V does not make everything safe: at fixed V the gap goes as 1/L by construction, and it duly returns 9 sigma.)   (6 candidate variables: 2 reach 3 sigma raw, 0 survive partialling on log V; strongest survivor is A_RC (DMS XI) at -2.1 sigma (raw -4.0).  N ~ 21, so |r| > 0.55 is needed for 3 sigma -- a weak constraint, quoted as one.  The sample's own Upsilon scatter is 0.336 dex against SPARC's 0.284 through the same estimator, so the DiskMass galaxies are not anomalously scattered either)
  
