----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART 5 -- what would close the gap: how many degrees, or how many dex
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ROUTE 1 -- inclination.  These discs are nearly face-on, so the deprojection 1/sin(i) is the sample's most fragile step: a +1 sigma inclination error moves the required Upsilon by -0.100 dex (median quoted error 2.9 deg).
                   A COHERENT inclination error of +8.1 degrees on the whole sample would bring the framework's requirement down to DiskMass's 0.31.  That is 2.8 times the typical quoted error, in the same direction for every galaxy.
                   SPARC is the control, and it does NOT support that route: over inclinations 30-90 degrees the full-curve Upsilon shows no inclination trend (r = +0.034, N = 120); the 30-45 degree galaxies give 0.517 against 0.543 above 60 degrees, 0.021 dex apart.  SPARC's floor is 30 degrees though, and half the DiskMass sample is below it, so this control does not reach where the trouble would be.
  ROUTE 2 -- a mass-to-light zero-point.  0.273 dex, coherent, and it is the SAME offset DiskMass already has against stellar populations (0.287 dex to Upsilon_K = 0.6).  The framework's requirement sits 0.013 dex from stellar populations and 0.273 dex from DiskMass.
  [PASS] 120e THE LOCALISATION, STATED.  The disagreement is not in a subset of galaxies and not in a property of the DiskMass sample: it is a single coherent number, and it is the SAME number DiskMass already disagrees with stellar population synthesis by.  The framework does not add a new tension -- it inherits the one that was already in the literature, and it sides with the population-synthesis half of it   (required 0.582 +- 0.126 (stat) +- 0.096 (RC form); distance to stellar populations -0.013 dex, to DiskMass +0.273 dex; DiskMass to stellar populations -0.287 dex.  The framework and stellar populations agree with each other far better than either agrees with DiskMass)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE LambdaCDM / NEWTONIAN ALTERNATIVE, COMPUTED BESIDE
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  DiskMass's own reading: at Upsilon_K = 0.31 a dark halo supplies 68% of the centripetal force at 2.2 h_R -- submaximal discs, comfortable for LambdaCDM, and the survey's published conclusion.
  Newton with NO halo on the same galaxies (maximum disc) needs Upsilon_K = 1.02, +0.23 dex above stellar populations; the kernel needs 0.582, -0.01 dex from them.  So the kernel does 43% of the work the halo would have to do, and the residual disagreement with DiskMass is what is left after that.
  the M/L-free cross-check: the framework's baryonic-to-total speed ratio at 2.2 h_R is 0.721 against DiskMass's measured 0.57 +- 0.07 -- the same disagreement, in the same direction, with no photometry involved at all

======================================================================================================================
MUTATION CONTROLS
======================================================================================================================
  [PASS] M120-1 the comparison is sensitive to a_0 and is not a rescaling any acceleration would pass: tripling a_0 moves the required Upsilon far enough to change the verdict, and in the direction that would REMOVE the disagreement -- so a wrong a_0 is a live escape route for the framework and is named as one, not hidden   (a_0 x 3 requires Upsilon_K = 0.301 against 0.582 (-0.29 dex); the a_0 that would put the requirement exactly on DiskMass's 0.31 is x2.88 canonical, i.e. 0.46 dex away when the two footings are only 0.082 dex apart -- so no admissible a_0 reconciles them, and an inadmissible one would wreck every other item in the hunt)
  [PASS] M120-2 shuffling the luminosities between galaxies destroys the per-galaxy correspondence but NOT the median, which is exactly what a flat offset predicts and is a further sign that nothing here is galaxy-specific.  The control that matters is the SPREAD: shuffling inflates the galaxy-to-galaxy scatter, so the estimator is reading real per-galaxy photometry and not noise   (shuffled median 0.706 vs true 0.582 (shuffle-to-shuffle spread 0.089 dex); scatter 0.692 dex shuffled against 0.336 true)
  [PASS] M120-3 the gas fraction, the one baryonic quantity not measured for these galaxies, is bracketed 10-30% and moves the answer by less than the disagreement, so no conclusion here rests on it   (required Upsilon_K = 0.612 (f_gas 0.10) to 0.547 (f_gas 0.30), a range of 0.064 against a gap of 0.272)
  [PASS] M120-4 the band conversion, which item 17 did not state, is carried and does not rescue anything.  Over the whole bracket the framework's requirement stays above DiskMass's value by more than a factor 1.4, and the like-for-like agreement with SPARC in check 120b holds at both ends   (Upsilon_K/Upsilon_[3.6] in (1.0, 1.3): the DiskMass requirement is 0.448-0.582 at 3.6 um against SPARC's 0.579, and 0.582 in K against DiskMass's 0.31 -- a factor 1.88 that the bracket cannot close)

======================================================================================================================
VERDICT
======================================================================================================================
  Item 120 asked where the disagreement with DiskMass lives.  The answer is: nowhere in particular, and that is the
  result.  (1) The overlap it was to be computed on is EMPTY and structurally must be -- the two surveys select on
  opposite ends of inclination.  (2) Running the identical estimator on SPARC, a disjoint sample, returns the same
  required mass-to-light ratio (0.58 at 3.6 um) as the DiskMass galaxies do (0.58 in K, 0.45-0.58 converted),
  so nothing about the DiskMass galaxies causes it, and the estimator that says so is validated in 120c -- it agrees
  with a full-curve fit of the same galaxies to 0.005 dex.  (3) Inside the sample, the two variables that do
  correlate with the gap at 3-4 sigma are the survey's own kinematic asymmetry measures, and both evaporate once the
  rotation speed -- which is algebraically inside the gap -- is held fixed; nothing else reaches 3 sigma.  (4) What is
  left is one coherent 0.27 dex offset, and it is the same offset DiskMass already carries against stellar
  population synthesis, which the framework agrees with to 0.01 dex.  Recorded AGAINST the framework's side of it:
  a coherent inclination error of +8.1 degrees on this nearly face-on sample would close the gap by itself, and SPARC
  cannot test that route because its own usable inclination floor is 30 degrees while 41% of the DiskMass
  sample lies below it.  Recorded FOR it: no admissible a_0 reconciles them (it would take x2.9), the gas fraction
  cannot (0.064 in Upsilon against a 0.272 gap), and the band conversion cannot (1.0-1.3 leaves a factor 1.4+).

